   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of Member States to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In 2022 the Government of Zimbabwe through Ministry of Higher and Tertiary Education, Innovation, Science and Technology and the Ministry of Public Service Labour and Social Welfare developed and adopted a sector-based Skills Anticipation Horticulture Sector Skills Strategy.
a. The Strategy was guided by the ILO Skills for Trade and Economic Diversification(STED) methodology.
b. The Horticulture Strategy was adopted by the Ministry of Industry and Commerce and Ministry of Lands, Agriculture, Water, Fisheries and Rural Development for implementation.
 1. The ILO through the ILO Skills Initiative for Africa(SIFA), a programme of the African Union Commission and African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD), assisted financially by the German Government and European Union(EU) provided financial and technical support to conduct and validate the study review and strategy.  The ILO also supported the establishment of a tripartite+ inter-ministerial task force-the National Task Team (NTT) to spearhead the Horticulture Sector Skills Review Study.</narrative>
   </description>
