   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.2. Increased capacity of Member States to strengthen skills and lifelong learning policies, governance models and financing system</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In 2022, the Government of Zimbabwe adopted a National Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) Policy.
a. The TVET Policy adopts the Education 5.0 strategy to channel education and training towards research, innovation, and industrialization and covers various elements of skills development, life-long learning and career development.
b. The policy has goals to improve the governance and quality assurance systems of the TVET, transitioning towards digitalisation and promoting integration of the needs of the private sector and the rural economy.


 1. ILO Skills Initiative for Africa(SIFA), a programme of the African Union Commission and African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD), assisted financially by the German Government and European Union(EU) and in collaboration with UNESCO provided financial and technical support to conduct policy consultations, the validation, technical reviews and publication of the National TVET Policy.</narrative>
   </description>
