  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of Member States to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In 2022, the Government of Zimbabwe through the Ministries of Higher and Tertiary Education, Innovation, Science and Technology, Public Service Labour and Social Welfare and social partners adopted a Mapping Report of  the institutional arrangements for labour market information (LMI) and skills anticipation
a. The mapping extensively reviewed existing literature on labour market information and skills anticipation; policy and legislative documents and frameworks, labour force surveys, establishment surveys, state of skills and skills audit reports, and reports on Technical Vocational Education and Training (TVET). 
b. The mapping also identified the structural(fragmentation) challenges that hindered the skills anticipation system from delivering expected results and being more responsive to the needs of the labour market.
2. The Government of Zimbabwe adopted a National Action Plan for Strengthening Labour Market Information Systems and Skills Anticipation Practices for 2022-2025.
a. The National Action Plan was adopted as framework to  coordinate efforts to strengthen the Labour Market Information (LMI), skills needs assessments and skills anticipation. 1.The ILO through the Skills Initiative for Africa (SIFA), a programme of the African Union Commission and African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD), assisted financially by the German Government and European Union provided financial and technical support to conduct, validate and publish the Mapping Report.
a. The ILO collaborated with a National Task Team (NTT) which constituted a tripartite-plus and inter-ministerial 10-member committee led by the Ministry of Higher and Tertiary Education, Innovation, Science and Technology Development, Ministry of Public Service, Labour and Social Welfare, Ministry of Industry and Commerce, Employer and Worker Organizations, Statistics Agency with Policy research institutions and academic institutions responsible for the design and conceptualisation of education and training provision.
b. The ILO supported the development of a Learning Brief on Strengthening Skills Anticipation Processes and Systems to share the experiences of countries(Zimbabwe, Zambia, Eswatini, Tanzania, Ghana and Gabon) that went through process of planning for strengthening Labour Market Information and Skills Anticipation Systems. 
2. The ILO through the Skills Initiative for Africa (SIFA), a programme of the African Union Commission and African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD), assisted financially by the German Government and European Union provided financial and technical support to develop and validate National Action Plan.</narrative>
   </description>
